## Why GPL-3.0

`sfpp/` contains transcriptions of Meshtastic firmware source, which is GPL-3.0:

| File | Relationship |
| --- | --- |
| `sfpp/pinsketch.py` | Transcription of `src/modules/Native/PinSketch.cpp`. Deliberately not a reimplementation: the simulator's sketches have to be the bytes the firmware puts on the air, and `sfpp/check_oracle.py` compiles the firmware's own source and diffs the two, case for case |
| `sfpp/sketchindex.py` | Port of `src/modules/Native/SketchIndex.cpp` |
| `sfpp/oracle.cpp` | Compiles the firmware's `PinSketch.cpp` directly, so the oracle is the shipping code rather than a copy of it |

Other modules under `sfpp/` cite firmware files for constants and behaviour - `MeshRadio.h`'s
`modemPresetToParams`, `RadioInterface.cpp` region-to-preset mapping, `NextHopRouter.cpp:297`,
`AdminModule.h`'s `kOutstandingAdminRequestMs`, `Default.h`'s congestion scaling, `NodeDB.cpp`'s
tiers. Those are descriptions of behaviour rather than copies of expression, and are cited so a
reader can check the model against the thing it models.

Meshtastic firmware: https://github.com/meshtastic/firmware

## Creative Commons work in this tree

Everything outside `sfpp/` - `lib/`, `presets/`, `loraMesh.py`, `batchSim.py`, `interactiveSim.py`,
`utils/`, `tests/` - is Meshtasticator, licensed **CC BY 4.0**:

> https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Part of that source is based on Spinsante, Gioacchini and Scalise, *A novel experimental-based tool
for the design of LoRa networks* (MetroInd4.0&IoT, 2019), which stems from Bor, Roedig, Voigt and
Alonso, *Do LoRa Low-Power Wide-Area Networks Scale?* (MSWiM '16) and the LoRaSim library.

The terrain, land-cover clutter, capture-aware physics, fitted link model and dynamic coding-rate
and TX-power policies are from Komzpa's Meshtasticator branch (PR #77, itself stacking powersjcb's
PR #33), merged with `meshtastic/Meshtasticator` master at `d1f64afc0`.

CC BY 4.0 carries no share-alike condition, so this material may be redistributed as part of a
GPL-3.0 work provided attribution is preserved - which is what this file exists to do. Contributions
to `lib/` that are not derived from the firmware remain Meshtasticator's work and are suitable for
sending upstream under its own licence; the four `lib/` fixes that arrived with the simulator are
kept as separate commits for exactly that reason.

## What the output is

A run's JSON, its text report and the explorer page are output of the program, not part of it. They
describe a simulation and carry no measurement of any real network.
